est","best","best","excellent","best","best","best","best","good","best","best","good","good","inaccuracy","good","best","good","inaccuracy","best","best","great","excellent","best","excellent","excellent","best","best","great","best","best","inaccuracy","good","best","great","best","best","good","best","inaccuracy","best","best","best","best","good","good","best","best","best","best","inaccuracy","good","good","excellent","best","inaccuracy","great","best","best","good","good","best","best","best","best","good","great","best","inaccuracy","excellent","excellent","inaccuracy","good","best","good","good","best","best","best","best","best","best","good","inaccuracy","best","best","best","best","best","best","best","best","great","inaccuracy","best","excellent","best","best","best"],"moments":[{"ply":20,"name":"10…Bxf3","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"10…Bxf3! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the knight on f3.","The next best move, 10…Bh5, would drop Black's winning chances from 46% to 18% (+4.04).","10…Bxf3 trades off the attacked bishop instead of losing it to hxg4.","White was threatening hxg4, winning a bishop for a pawn. The bishop takes something with it instead.","Trades bishop for knight.","The bishop on f3 can be taken by the bishop on e2 or the g2 pawn."]},{"ply":45,"name":"23.fxe5","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"23.fxe5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Takes the pawn on e5.","Moving away uncovers the bishop on e3, which now hits the h6 pawn.","The next best move, 23.h5, would drop White's winning chances from 51% to 37% (−1.46).","23.fxe5 wins a pawn.","Takes the e5 pawn and wins a pawn once the captures settle.","Creates a passed pawn on e5: no enemy pawn can stop it any more."]},{"ply":52,"name":"26…Qf6","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"26…Qf6! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Attacks the c3 pawn, which is undefended.","Shores up the f5 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1).","The next best move, 26…a6, would drop Black's winning chances from 46% to 35% (+1.64).","26…Qf6 threatens …Qxc3+, winning a pawn.","It threatens …Qxc3+, winning a pawn, so White has to spend the next move on it.","White answers 27.Kb2."]},{"ply":58,"name":"29…Qb6+","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"29…Qb6+! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The queen joins the attack: it now hits 2 squares around the White king.","Moves the attacked queen to safety.","The next best move, 29…Qe6, would drop Black's winning chances from 50% to 24% (+3.17).","29…Qb6+ joins the attack on the king and saves the attacked queen.","Gives check, so the reply is forced.","White answers 30.Ka1."]},{"ply":80,"name":"40…Nc6","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"40…Nc6! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Moves the attacked knight to safety.","Protects the a7 pawn, which was hanging.","The next best move, 40…Nc4, would drop Black's winning chances from 69% to 54% (−0.44).","40…Nc6 gets the knight out of the way of Qxe5.","White was threatening Qxe5, winning a knight. The knight steps away.","White answers 41.Be3."]},{"ply":90,"name":"45…Nc6","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"45…Nc6! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Moves the attacked knight to safety.","Brings the knight, Black's least active piece, into play (from 2 to 5 usable squares).","The next best move, 45…Qe8, would drop Black's winning chances from 70% to 54% (−0.39).","45…Nc6 gets the knight out of the way of Qxd8.","White was threatening Qxd8, winning a knight. The knight steps away.","White answers 46.Kc1."]},{"ply":116,"name":"58…Ne5","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"58…Ne5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Centralises the knight on e5, where it reaches the most squares.","The next best move, 58…Nd8, would drop Black's winning chances from 85% to 54% (−0.45).","58…Ne5 centralises the knight.","White answers 59.Bxa7, winning a pawn.","After 59.Bxa7 h4 60.Kc3 h3 61.Bg1 Ka3, Black is a pawn down (−4.69)."]}],"opening":{"name":"Robatsch Defense: Rossolimo Variation","eco":"A41","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 5.Be2: 9 moves in all, 5 by White and 4 by Black.","Black left the book with 5…Nd7. In 111 games from this position, strong players played 5…Nc6 (67%), 5…Bxf3 (22%), 5…c5 (5%) or 5…Nf6 (4%).","The main line goes on 5…Nc6 6.Be3 e5 7.d5 Bxf3 8.Bxf3 Nd4 9.Nc3 c5 10.dxc6.","From here White scored 48% in those games, with 17% drawn."]}}